Introduction
Gastric cancer (GC) is one of the most frequently diagnosed cancers worldwide. There were almost one million new cases and 660,000 deaths in 2022. Consequently, gastric cancer was listed as the fifth most prevalent and the fifth deadliest cancer globally [
1
]. Early diagnosis can reduce mortality rates and improve patient outcomes [
2
]. In high-risk countries, cancer screening programs with endoscopic and radiographic methods enable early detection in asymptomatic patients [
3
]. For early-stage gastric cancer patients, endoscopy resection and surgery are the standard treatment options while for advanced stages, chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy are commonly used [
2
,
4
,
5
].
Artificial intelligence (AI) has greatly impacted clinical practice in recent years. Accurate diagnosis of early gastric cancer (EGC) lesions, the prediction of patient prognosis and metastases, and effective treatments are possible with AI-based approaches [
6
–
8
]. Machine learning (ML) is a promising subfield of AI. ML models can accurately forecast patient outcomes and drive insights by identifying patterns in patient data [
9
–
11
]. ML also assists physicians on image diagnosis, reducing interpretation variability, and diagnostic errors [
12
]. Deep learning (DL) is a popular ML technique in computer vision for healthcare. Convolutional neural network (CNN) which is specifically designed to process data with a grid-like topology, such as images, can be implemented to analyze magnifying endoscopic images and EGC detection [
13
–
15
].
Common traditional medical procedures for GC detection such as endoscopy and gastroscopy have drawbacks. They are invasive, expensive, and not widely accessible [
16
]. There is a risk of endoscopy failing to detect GC due to inattentiveness of personnel, errors or patient discomfort [
17
]. Hence, widely applicable and less invasive tools with precise GC diagnosis such as blood and tongue coating sampling are required [
18
–
20
]. Utilization of high-throughput omics data including proteomics and.
metabolomics helps in understanding biological pathways and molecular features for GC detection through non-invasive sampling [
20
,
21
].
Despite the advancements in AI for cancer detection, there are still limitations. ML models can face over-fitting and under-fitting challenges and need external dataset validation [
22
]. Although AI has advanced in cancer detection, certain limitations remain [
22
]. Additionally, DL models are prone to systematic errors due to heterogeneity and imbalance in GC datasets [
23
,
24
]. Batch effects, feature selection, feature extraction, and classification are common challenges when dealing with omics datasets [
25
,
26
]. Batch effects can cause data misinterpretation and irreproducibility in cancer research. One study demonstrated how incorrect classification due to batch effects resulted in improper cancer treatment [
27
]. Therefore, batch effects need to be accurately identified and corrected [
27
]. Feature selection problems impact the discovery of cancer biomarkers and the performance of predicting models [
28
]. Common feature selection methods can lead to over-fitting, high costs and a disregard for feature correlation or feature interactions. So, It is important to use methods suitable for omics studies [
28
,
29
].
Despite recent progress in gastric cancer prediction, the integration of AI with less invasive diagnostic methods to identify reliable biomarkers remains under-explored. In this paper, we propose a new method called World Hyper Fuzzy Deep Learning (WHFDL) to address the existing limitations mentioned. Our primary objective in this research is to accurately predict gastric cancer using metabolomics data from blood plasma samples. The proposed method consists of two major steps: feature selection and classification. To address the high dimensionality of metabolomics data and avoid overfitting and lower computational costs, we implemented a World Hyper Heuristic algorithm for feature selection. This approach leverages reinforcement learning to dynamically integrate multiple meta-heuristics and select the most relevant features for gastric cancer prediction. Additionally, to precisely classify the inherently noisy and ambiguous data, we employed a Hierarchical Fused Fuzzy Deep Neural Network, which combines fuzzy logic and deep learning in a unified architecture. By optimizing the entire model through hyper-heuristic-based feature selection and fuzzy deep learning, our method achieved an accuracy of 94%. The contributions of this study are as follows:
Accurate non-invasive diagnosis of gastric cancer using metabolomics data.
Hyper-heuristic-based feature selection for dynamic exploration and exploitation through reinforcement learning.
Identification and presentation of six metabolites as a biomarker panel for gastric cancer diagnosis, based on hierarchical fuzzy deep learning.
Achieved an accuracy of 94% and an AUC of 93% based on the ROC curve in gastric cancer diagnosis.
The integration of World Hyper Heuristic, Hierarchical Fused Fuzzy Deep Neural Network, and metabolomics data improved interpretability and enhanced biological insights.
Combining these approaches increased reliability and robustness of gastric cancer diagnosis compared to conventional methods.
This paper is organized as follows: Section “
Literature review
” provides a review of related works, highlighting existing methods and their applications in GC field. Section “
World hyper fuzzy deep learning for gastric cancer prediction
” details the methodology implemented in our study, including the feature selection process and the integration of fuzzy logic with deep neural networks for classification. Section “
Experimental evaluation
” outlines the experimental evaluations, providing an analysis of the model's performance. Section “
Discussion
” offers a discussion on the results, including insights into the strengths and challenges of the proposed method. Finally, Section “
Conclusion
” concludes the paper with the key findings and highlights potential directions for future research.
Literature review
This section explores diverse data types and ML methods applied for GC prediction. Data processed and then utilized for this purpose can be collected through invasive and non-invasive sampling methods. Section “
Omics data
”, highlights recent studies on ML applications for omics data including multi-omics, proteomics, and metabolomics, which are collected through less-invasive sampling methods. Section “
Imaging data
”, focuses on non-invasive imaging data and ML models developed for GC detection and prediction. Finally, Section “
Invasive approaches
”, presents ML approaches using omics data and imaging data acquired through invasive sampling. In Fig.
1
, the types of data collected are illustrated.
Fig. 1
Data types for gastric cancer prediction
Omics data
Omics data, including multi-omics, proteomics, and metabolomics, can be obtained via non-invasive methods for GC detection and diagnosis [
30
]. Multi-omics integrates two or more large-scale omics datasets (genomics, metagenomics, transcriptomics, proteomics, etc.) to identify biomarkers and improve disease diagnosis and prognosis [
31
]. For example, a study [
32
] applied a Random Forest Deep Feature Selection (RDFS) algorithm combined with neural networks on gastric cancer multi-omics data, achieving high prediction accuracy. In another study [
33
], GMPS, an ML model based on a Programmed Cell Death Signature and incorporating Tumor Mutational Burden, was developed using multi-omics data to predict gastric cancer outcomes and support treatment planning. Oral microbiota, a key subset of metagenomics, has also gained attention in GC research. A deep neural network model (OmNN) based on oral microbiota [
34
] predicted GC prognosis and risk, finding that higher survival rates were associated with elevated Aggregatibacter and reduced Filifactor and Moryella levels, allowing accurate prediction of 3-year and 5-year survival rates (AUC of 0.814 and 0.912, respectively). Another study [
35
] identified six bacterial genera associated with GC using ML methods (RF, GBM, GLM), with Haemophilus and Neisseria more abundant in cancer patients and Leuconostoc, Streptococcus, Abiotrophia, and Prevotella less common. Metabolomics analyzes micromolecular metabolites to understand cancer progression through metabolic pathways [
21
,
36
]; one study [
21
] combined plasma metabolomics with ML (LASSO and Random Forest) to develop a diagnostic model achieving an AUROC of 0.96. Proteomics similarly contributes to cancer prediction and treatment by identifying key proteins that support and regulate cancer cells [
37
]. For instance, a metaproteomics analysis of tongue-coating samples [
20
] identified 50 microbial proteins, which were used to build a highly precise GBM model to classify individuals with and without GC.
Imaging data
Prior research [
38
] explored non-invasive GC prediction by capturing tongue images and extracting features such as tongue shape, saliva, tongue coating thickness, and texture, using deep learning to isolate tongue areas and employing an EfficientNet CNN for prediction, achieving 93.6% accuracy. Capsule endoscopy, an ingestible pill with built-in imaging sensors, light, and transmitter, allows safe gastrointestinal screening [
39
]. Studies [
40
,
41
] developed automatic systems using CNNs and faster RCNNs trained on over 1 million images to detect gastric focal lesions, submucosal tumors, polyps, and ulcers, demonstrating high sensitivity and specificity. Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is another non-invasive technique suitable for GC detection and staging due to its clear visualization of stomach layers [
42
]; by integrating Backprojection (BP) and Support Vector Machines (SVM), researchers improved image processing and tumor recognition, achieving staging accuracies of 80.55% (T1), 76.15% (T2), 96.44% (T3), and 62.45% (T4).
Invasive approaches
Invasive methods, such as endoscopy, remain widely used for gastric cancer (GC) diagnosis despite significant advances in non-invasive omics and imaging approaches [
43
,
44
]. Proteomics analysis of surgically resected stomach tissue has been employed to classify GC subtypes and predict prognosis, with a deep learning framework (AE) identifying key protein features and Consensus Clustering defining two high-risk subtypes, S-I and S-II, which were then used to train supervised models including Random Forest (RF), Logistic Regression (LR), and Support Vector Machines (SVM) [
45
]. Deep learning has also been applied to invasive medical imaging, such as Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)-based systems integrated with magnifying endoscopy with narrow-band imaging (M-NBI) for early GC detection. Inception-v3 models achieved high sensitivity (91.18%) in distinguishing cancerous from non-cancerous lesions on 371 endoscopic images [
46
], while transfer learning with deep residual networks on over 14 million images produced a CNN-CAD system with 98.7% diagnostic accuracy on 2300 M-NBI images [
13
]. Furthermore, deep learning models trained on histologic images of surgically removed gastric adenocarcinoma successfully detected immunotherapy-sensitive subtypes, including EBV-positive and MSI/dMMR tumors, achieving an AUC of 0.947 [
47
]. Despite great potential, Endoscopic procedures and the collection of tissue samples through surgery for further analysis are uncomfortable for patients compared to non-invasive methods and they carry risks of complications. A longer time for recovery is required after these procedures, contributing to the costly nature of endoscopy and surgery. Also, CNNs heavily rely on the quality of the images so inconsistencies in procedures can lead to a poor performance of the model.
World hyper fuzzy deep learning for gastric cancer prediction
This section presents the overall workflow of our proposed World Hyper Fuzzy Deep Learning (WHFDL) approach for GC prediction. Section “
Overview of method, patients and dataset
”, introduces the metabolomics dataset used in this research, detailing the patient cohorts as well as the collection and processing of plasma samples. Section “
Feature selection
” then presents the feature selection process and highlights its importance. It further discusses the implementation of the World hyper-heuristic approach for feature selection, outlining the three main phases involved. Finally, Section “
Classifications
” outlines the classification process, which utilizes a Hierarchical Fused Fuzzy Deep Learning to perform the GC prediction. This section also covers the four key components of the mentioned architecture, along with the details of its training and optimization procedures.
Overview of method, patients and dataset
The dataset [
21
] utilized in this study consists of plasma-targeted metabolomics profiles, collected from 702 individuals across three independent cohorts, including 389 gastric cancer (GC) patients and 313 non-gastric cancer (NGC) controls. Plasma samples were obtained from two Major cancer hospitals in China and were collected before any anti-cancer treatment. Cohort 1 consisted of 145 GC patients (71% Male, median age of 60 years, Stage I/II/III/IV: 52/30/53/10) and 281 NGC individuals (52% Male, median age of 50 years). Cohort 2 (
n
= 95) included 63 GC patients (76% Male, median age of 64 years, Stage I/II/III/IV: 16/14/24/9) and 32 NGC controls (88% Male, median age of 51 years). Cohort 3 only consisted of GC patients (63% Male, median age of 60 years; Stage I/II/III/IV: 40/44/89/8) and follow-up data intended for prognosis analysis were available in addition to its plasma targeted metabolomics data. This prognosis data was reported as a median follow-up period of 40 months, lacking detailed longitudinal recorded required for time-series prediction. Therefore, we excluded the follow-up data and instead utilized the metabolomics data from all three cohorts as input to our WHFDL model for GC prediction.
Plasma samples from all three cohorts underwent a standardized targeted metabolomics workflow based on liquid chromatography–mass spectrometry (LC–MS). Following overnight fasting, blood was collected by peripheral venous sampling for Cohorts 1 and 2, and from the superior vena cava for Cohort 3. In order to isolate plasma, the collected blood was further processed using a two-step centrifugation protocol and was then stored at − 80
