of plasma was mixed with ice-cold methanol, vortexed, centrifuged, and the resulting clear supernatant was dried and reconstituted in a suitable solution so it could be analyzed by the LC–MS instrument. The separation of metabolites was done using a reverse-phase UPLC column, which organizes compounds based on their chemical properties. To facilitate detection of specific metabolites, the separated metabolites were then quantified using a high-sensitivity triple quadrupole mass spectrometer (AB QTRAP 6500 +) operated in multiple reaction monitoring (MRM) mode. The samples were also processed in a randomized and blinded manner during data acquisition.
To ensure analytical consistency, a quality control (QC) protocol was implemented, which involved inserting a pooled plasma sample after every ten patient samples during the LC–MS run. Metabolite signals in samples were then normalized using the ratio of global to local QC peak areas, producing relative abundance values. As a result, metabolite quantities are expressed in relation to one another rather than as absolute concentrations. Following this protocol, a total of 147 metabolites, including amino acids, organic acids, acylcarnitines, amines, nucleosides, vitamins, nucleotides and carbohydrates, were detected across all 702 plasma samples. Importantly, since the dataset was normalized and processed according to a specific QC-guided pipeline, any future application of our model requires new data to be generated and preprocessed using the same procedures described in the original study.
Using the plasma-targeted metabolomics dataset described above, we subsequently performed data analysis and applied a World Hyper-Heuristic strategy for feature selection to identify the most informative and relevant subset of features. This reinforcement learning–based method not only optimized feature selection but also contributed to handling batch effects present in the dataset. The data classification was then done using a Hierarchical Fused Fuzzy Deep Neural Network, which integrates the strengths of deep learning and fuzzy logic to effectively overcome the noise and uncertainty problems in metabolomics data.
Feature selection
Feature selection is a crucial step in analyzing high-dimensional datasets, aiming to enhance model performance and interpretability by identifying a relevant, efficient, and less noisy subset of attributes [
48
,
49
]. Traditional approaches, including filter, wrapper, and embedded methods [
50
], often suffer from overfitting and high computational costs [
51
], which can be mitigated using meta-heuristic algorithms such as evolutionary algorithms and swarm intelligence [
51
,
52
]. In this work, we employ a World Hyper-Heuristic (WHH) [
53
], which integrates multiple meta-heuristics with reinforcement learning to dynamically balance exploration and exploitation, select the most relevant features, and avoid premature convergence. Feature selection is formulated as an optimization problem to maximize performance while minimizing redundancy [
54
]. Candidate subsets are initialized either randomly or heuristically [
55
], evaluated using relevant metrics (e.g., accuracy, F1-score, RMSE) with possible penalty terms to control complexity [
56
]. These subsets are then refined iteratively through meta-heuristic operators such as selection, crossover, mutation in genetic algorithms [
57
,
58
] or velocity-position updates in particle swarm optimization [
59
] until termination criteria are met [
60
]. This integrated approach improves classification accuracy, reduces dimensionality, and adapts effectively to dataset-specific characteristics [
52
,
61
].
The World hyper-heuristic algorithm effectively optimizes search problems by leveraging a reinforcement learning (RL) method and combining multiple meta-heuristics. It can dynamically adjust exploration and exploitation and follows two main steps: rewarding and selecting meta-heuristics. In rewarding step, WHH evaluates different meta-heuristics based on their performance in finding optimal solutions, ensuring a fair comparison between them. In selection step, it decides the most promising meta-heuristic for next iteration while maintaining a balance between diversity in search and favoring high-reward meta-heuristics. This approach prevents the algorithm from getting stuck in a local optimum while gradually shifting towards exploiting the best performing meta-heuristics over iterations. WHH consists of three key phases: the first phase is initialization of hyper-parameters, the second focuses on rewarding meta-heuristics and the third involves iterative selection and optimization. These three phases will be explained in the following, reported as three algorithms.
In this article, we implemented eight optimization algorithms as a subset of WHH, which are: Biogeography Optimization (BO) [
62
], Differential Evolution (DE) [
63
], Firefly Algorithm (FFA) [
64
], Harmony Search (HS) [
65
], Improved Invasive Weed Optimization (IIWO) [
66
], Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) [
67
], Simulated Annealing (SA) [
68
], and Teaching–Learning Optimization Algorithm (TLOA) [
69
]. In Appendix
1
, the hyperparameters of optimization algorithms are represented by the WHH algorithm [
53
].
