], where the data classification is performed. This architecture addresses both the ambiguity and noise inherent in gastric cancer metabolomics data by combining fuzzy logic and deep neural representations. A Hierarchical Fused Fuzzy Deep learning consists of four core components: a fuzzy-representation branch that captures class ambiguity, a deep-representation branch, which handles noises and provides much cleaner data, a fusion module that integrates the two views, and a task-driven classification layer. Each input vector is processed along two parallel paths. The fuzzy representation part maps each raw feature to membership degrees and the neural branch extracts higher-level features to reduce noise. These two representations are joined in the fusion block and then forwarded to a task-driven layer, where the final class labels are assigned. Throughout the description, the symbol
