Experimental evaluation
In this section of the paper, the evaluation of the experimental results and their comparisons are discussed. First, the performance of feature selection is examined in subsection “
Feature selection experimental evaluation
”. Then, in two parts, the evaluation of traditional and modern methods is proposed in order and their accuracy is examined. Finally, Subsection “
Classification experimental evaluation
” evaluates the classification methods. All thirteen classifiers were implemented using the hyperparameter settings determined by the Taguchi Method [
72
], as described Table 9 in Appendix
2
.
Feature selection experimental evaluation
Given the high dimensionality of our metabolomics dataset, feature selection is an undeniably important step, as it directly impacts the model’s accuracy and predictive power by determining the quality of features provided for training. In this study, we aimed to identify the most relevant, efficient, and informative features to minimize overfitting, enhance model interpretability and computational efficiency. We reviewed four major categories of feature selection methods, including Filter methods, Wrapper methods, Embedded methods and Hybrid models
,
as illustrated in Fig.
4
. World Hyper Heuristic (WHH) falls under the Hybrid category, which combines multiple metaheuristics and uses a fitness function based on classifier accuracy to guide the feature selection process and adapt to the complexity of metabolomics data. By including both classical and more recent state-of-the-art methods, we ensured a broad and meaningful comparison across feature selection strategies.
Fig. 4
Feature selection methods employed in this study, classified into filter, wrapper, embedded and hybrid categories
To evaluate the performance of five feature selection methods, we used the selected feature subsets as input to multiple classification models and assessed their predictive accuracy according to Eq.
14
. This evaluation was conducted for each of the feature selection algorithms, utilizing both traditional machine learning and deep learning classifiers. We also examined whether the selected features aligned with existing medical knowledge about gastric cancer biomarkers to ensure their biological and clinical relevance. Furthermore, for our proposed WHFDL model, we analyzed feature importance using a permutation-based approach, helping us identify which features had the greatest contribution to our model's predictive capability.
The resulted classification accuracies are discussed in the following sections. Section “
Performance of traditional and statistical methods with feature selection
” presents the performance of feature selection algorithms in combination with traditional and statistical models, while Section “
Performance of deep learning models with feature selection
” evaluates their performance alongside deep learning architectures. Additionally, in Table
1
, all feature selection algorithms in conjunction with traditional and statistical models and deep learning models are proposed.
