100
], its relevance to the model appears mechanistically plausible. Additionally, variations in Hypoxanthine levels in serum from gastric cancer patients [
104
] further support its biological significance in the disease. Together, our model's ranking of feature importance is in line with existing biological and clinical data in showing that the model is effectively identifying notable metabolic changes associated with gastric cancer.
There are several limitations to consider. A key constraint stems from the relative quantification of metabolites in the dataset, as described in the methodology of [
21
]. The study’s quality control (QC) process involved the creation of pooled plasma QC samples, which were derived from all patients in the study. These QC samples were interspersed strategically between batches of patient samples during mass spectrometry analysis. This process ensured instrument stability, as each batch included a QC sample after every ten patient samples. Deviations observed in the metabolite levels of these theoretically identical QC samples across different runs were then used to normalize the corresponding patient data. While this normalization process is critical for maintaining data integrity, it results in metabolite levels being expressed relative to one another rather than as absolute concentrations. Consequently, the clinical applicability and generalizability of models trained on this dataset are inherently limited. The model's performance is specifically calibrated to the patterns found in data processed using this particular QC and normalization method. Therefore, for the model to be applied to an independent dataset, the data generation and processing pipeline, including the use of identical pooled QC samples, would need to be replicated. This reliance on a specific in-house data processing method poses a challenge for broader clinical application, as the results are primarily intended to serve as proof-of-concept for using machine learning-based approaches to detect cancer using metabolite profiles generated in this exact manner. In general, the limitations are divided into the following concepts:
Relative quantification
QC-based normalization
Dataset specificity
Replication requirement
Proof-of-concept scope
A principal delimitation of this study is its focus on identifying key metabolic features directly associated with gastric cancer, achieved through innovative heuristic methodologies. Once these potentially relevant metabolites were identified, the study aimed to create a robust and accurate classification model for gastric cancer detection. To enhance model interpretability, feature importance analysis was used to assess the contribution of each selected metabolite. This process prioritizes biologically significant features and creates a model framework that is more transparent and interpretable, focusing the study’s analysis on specific objectives related to gastric cancer detection.
There are several promising directions for future research based on the results of this study. One important avenue is the development of more advanced computational approaches to overcome the limitations posed by the current relative normalization technique. This could involve exploring batch effect removal algorithms or incorporating external reference standards to facilitate a more straightforward and generalizable estimation of metabolite concentrations. Another exciting opportunity lies in the integration of multi-omics data. Future studies could incorporate proteomic data from similar cohorts, leveraging federated learning platforms to jointly train predictive models on various datasets. This decentralized machine learning approach would enable the identification of synergistic biomarkers and enhance diagnostic performance while respecting privacy. Investigating such integrative strategies would not only deepen our understanding of the biological mechanisms underlying gastric cancer, but also facilitate the development of more clinically translatable diagnostic tools.
Conclusion
In this study, we present a novel method for non-invasive gastric cancer prediction using the World Hyper Fuzzy Deep Learning (WHFDL) approach. The WHFDL method is composed of two main stages: first, metabolomics data undergoes dimensionality reduction via the World Hyper Heuristic (WHH) feature selector; and second, a Hierarchical Fused Fuzzy Deep Neural Network is employed for classification and training. The key advantage of the WHH feature selection is its reinforcement learning-based approach, which autonomously selects the most relevant features. Also, the main reason for implementing a Hierarchical Fused Fuzzy Deep Neural Network is the interpretable approach and following fuzzy rules and logic in data classification.
The effectiveness of WHFDL was evaluated through a comprehensive comparison with 17 different methods, encompassing a broad spectrum of classical and modern approaches. This evaluation covered four feature selection methods and 13 other classification models, with performance metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score, macro and weighted averages, as well as receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves, area under the curve (AUC) scores and the confusion matrix of the methods. Six metabolic features were extracted for the diagnosis of gastric cancer in this study, the most important of which are Methyl adenosine, Carnitine, Guanidine acetic acid, Hypoxanthine, NMN, and Succinate. According to the experimental results, the best macro average of F1-scores, recalls, and precisions were obtained using the proposed method, as 94%, 93% and 94%, respectively. Also, the best weighted average of F1-scores, recalls, and precisions were 94%. Finally, the Accuracy of the method and the area under the curve (AUC) extracted from the receiver operating characteristic curve (ROC) were 94% and 0.9384, respectively.
Beyond the predictive performance, WHFDL was interpreted through a series of analyses designed to assess interpretability, calibration, and robustness, which are crucial for deployment in sensitive domains such as clinical diagnostics. SHAP and LIME analyses consistently identified six features as the most influential predictors, highlighting coherence in both global and local interpretability and supporting the relevance of the selected biomarkers. Calibration results indicated a generally reliable alignment between predicted probabilities and actual outcomes, with minor overconfidence in higher probability ranges, which is an expected pattern in medical classification tasks. Robustness evaluation using FGSM perturbations (ε = 0.05) showed a reduction in accuracy from 94 to 76%, which is a moderate expected drop in models not subjected to adversarial training. These evaluations provide a multidimensional understanding of the model’s behavior and reinforce WHFDL’s applicability in real-world scenarios where trust, transparency, and resilience are as vital as predictive power.
Although the proposed method has achieved significant results, it has several limitations. There is a problem with data quality control in this method, and in future work to generate new data, the studied instructions for data generation must be followed. Also, the problem of reducing data dimensions and the problem of batch effects in various data are other limitations of the datasets in this study. For future work, data collection using multiple omics data modalities, implementation of publicly available online methods, and resolution of data quality issues should be considered. Additionally, while the model includes interpretability and robustness analyses, minor miscalibration at high probability levels and moderate vulnerability to adversarial perturbations suggest areas for further refinement. Applying post-hoc calibration techniques and incorporating adversarial training could further enhance its reliability in clinical deployment.
These findings not only demonstrate the effectiveness of WHFDL in gastric cancer prediction but also highlight its potential to support clinical decision-making, provided that data quality and validation challenges are addressed. Future research will focus on validating the method across independent cohorts and exploring its integration into clinical workflows to move closer to practical adoption.
